# Chats

**The transcript is a window.** A chat opens on its newest page (`Settings.historyPageSize`, capped at `REMOTE_HISTORY_PAGE` in a browser through the gateway: `App` passes `transcriptPageCap`) and pages older through `beforeSeq`. Anything that describes the whole thread, a call conversation or an agent is read from the daemon, never folded from loaded `items` nor bounded by a capped listing (`.claude/rules/run-statistics.md`):

- Run state (`useRunState`, `GET /v1/chats/:runId/state`).
- Spend (`useChatTotals`, and the nodes route for teams).
- The timeline, artifacts list, task-list words (`withDurableTaskLists`), shells (`withDurableShells`), delegates (`withDurableDelegates`), and node liveness (`withDurableNodeStatus`).
- Worked time and tool count, which take the daemon's figure alone where it has one. A call card's clock is the daemon's `callFigures[].clock`, continued over the rows above its `throughSeq` (`chats/call-clock.ts`, twin of the daemon's `utils/call-clock.ts`): the call's open time, its waits on its own monitors and calls included and its waits on the user left out, so it runs while the card says `waiting` and is never replaced when a turn lands.
- In each case the turn in flight is added on top (`withLiveCost`, `openTurns`).

A team run's window is its OWN flow only:

- Every window PAGE read passes `conversation=root` (the daemon answers a chat, whose agent calls no one, with the whole run). A root page anchors what each call's FOLDED card is drawn from. The tail catch-up and the reconnect replay read the whole run since a cursor.
- A row whose `inCall` is a call id (`isCallRow`; the run's own flow carries null) never joins `items`. Both places a row can arrive split on it into `callRows` — `use-run-stream.ts`'s `ingest` (live rows and the replay) and `use-chat-run.ts`'s `catchUpTail` — and the stream's `structure` folds the anchors and the call rows as one. An opened call card reads its own conversation through `CallConversationPager` (`call=<id>`). What it read is forgotten on thread open and close, and on return to the tail, whose root pages skipped the calls' rows written meanwhile.
- A reader about a call's turns, cards, shells or pages reads `transcriptRows` (the window plus every structure row filed under a call — a page's card anchors, live rows, an opened card's pages), never `items` alone.

Anchors and run state:

- Anchors (`GET …/items` → `{items, anchors}`) are kept apart from `items`, laid in only where the transcript is folded, and never drawn as rows.
- Live rows ask for anchors through `chats/use-live-anchor-asks.ts`.
- Whatever replaces anchors calls `forget`. A page landing after a live answer merges.
- Rows refused by `addItem`'s away-from-tail guard bring no anchors.
- Never rebuild a call heuristically from its later rows: it draws ghost cards.
- A call row whose card is not in the fold yet (a live row ahead of its asked-for start) is drawn nowhere, never as its callee's block in the run's own flow (`isUnplacedCallRow`); only `UNCLAIMABLE_KINDS` stay in that flow.
- `useRunState` re-reads on open, reconnect, a structural row or a status announce, never on an activity phrase. The window supplies details; the daemon decides what has ENDED: a shell, delegate or call the page reads as running that began at or before the read's `throughSeq` and that the read says is over, is over (`withoutDaemonEndedShells`, `applyDaemonReadings` in `durable-transcript.ts`, `callEndedPerDaemon`). The same read carries each delegate's whole-run tool count, which `applyDaemonReadings` lays on its card with the delegate's rows above `throughSeq` added. The open turns' unanswered calls (`openToolCalls`) are folded in with the page, so a foreground command shows while the reader is elsewhere. Open requests off the tail are the daemon's too.

Jumps (search, global search, timeline, the working-agents card) — `chats/use-transcript-jump.ts`, DOM contract in `chats/jump-target.ts`:

- Every row a jump can land on carries its seq (`data-transcript-seq` / `data-row-seq` / `data-call-seq`), a second row it draws (`data-seq-also`, a tool row's result) and a page-unique `data-jump-key`; the mark is matched on the key, never the seq (a turn and its first row share one). The anchor drawing the hit wins, innermost first; only without one, the nearest start below it.
- The folds a row is drawn inside (tool group, tool row, long reasoning, sub-agent block, call card) are found from the transcript's STRUCTURE (`chats/jump-folds.ts`), never from seq ranges — interleaved delegates span each other's seqs. They are opened through thread memory, then the row is landed on.
- A row filed under a call is located (one-row read), the window paged to its card, the card and every card around it opened and read back to the row (`loadThrough`), then the row is marked inside the card.

One home for each concern:

- The per-run stream is `chats/use-run-stream.ts`.
- A conversation drawn outside the chat screen — the builder's chat dock, a thread's or a team's analysis popup — is the chat screen itself: `Chats` with `hosted` (`chats/hosted-thread.ts`). Its run list is the one run the surface's `open` answers (`useChatRun`'s `pinned`), and it draws the thread alone: no list, no new-chat composer, no notifications, archive sweep, global search or Analyze. The surface gives its header controls, empty text, composer label and queue scope, and is told back through `onBeforeSend` (before every turn it starts: a message, a button press, a recommendations Apply, a Retry — `startHostedTurn`), `onWorkingChange`, `onAwaitingChange` and `onTurnSettled` (as a turn's ending arrives, before a queued message leaves behind it: `useChatRun`'s `onTurnEndRef`). `AnalysisDialog` takes the chat screen as `renderThread`, so the two modules do not import each other. Never write a second chat surface; a hosted surface that needs something is given it through `HostedThread`. The one exception is a call's Show all popup (`call-conversation-dialog.tsx`), whose scope is one call rather than a run, with no composer: its foot is the call card's own (`CallConversationFooter`, the message box and figures `CallBlock` draws).
- Run-row fields from `run_status` are read by `chats/run-status-row.ts`.
- The message queue rules are in `chats/use-message-queue.ts`. Every queue persists (`queued-message-store.ts`): the chat screen's under one key, a hosted conversation's under its surface's own (`HostedThread.queueScope`), so closing the dock or a popup, or a reload, loses nothing — it goes out once the surface is back and its agent is free.
- `joinRun`/`leaveRun` both live in `use-chat-run.ts` (`activateRun`/`deactivateRun`), which holds one join of the open run (`heldRoomRef`). `DaemonClient` counts joins per run, so several chat screens share the socket's room and it is left only when the last one lets go.
- The chat screen's composer text is a store (`chats/composer-text.ts`), never state on `Chats`: the field subscribes to the text (`MessageComposer`'s `text`), the screen only to facts it draws (`useComposerTextFact`: is there text, the `/` query), and send paths read `get()` at the press. Typing must not re-render the screen (pinned in `Chats.spec.tsx`).
- `Chats` stays mounted across nav switches. Read settings at the moment a run is created (`renderer/run-settings.ts` → `currentRunSettings()`, the one reader every renderer run start uses: chat, team run, analysis, a card's run, the builder's chat; main's autopilot normalises through the same `runSettingsOf` in `shared/contracts.ts`), never from cached state. Re-read lists such as fast actions when the screen becomes active again.

**Sending and the queue:**

- The whole send/queue decision is `followUpDelivery`/`parkedReason` in `chats/follow-up-delivery.ts`, pinned row by row in its spec. The send path, button label, hover sentence and placeholder all read it.
- Principle: a message goes straight in when the agent will read it soon, and queues when the agent is busy with something that blocks reading.
- `sendFollowUp` queues when the agent is working or when the queue is non-empty. Never decide on `streaming` alone.
- Queueing behind an idle run kicks the drain. Never kick it while the agent is working.
- RUN_BUSY from the daemon means queue and retry. The daemon is the fact, and the renderer's `streaming` is only a belief. A Send-now it refuses draws the daemon's sentence on its row (`SteerStatus.reason`).
- The composer refuses no geniro command itself.
- Whether a unit is foreground or background is the CLI's own word, as announced by the daemon. Never guess it.
- A Stop leaves the queue standing on every surface, the hosted chats included: only `settleReleasesQueue` decides whether a settle drains. The head waits for its Send.
- A held turn (`holdingFor`) is not a working agent. The live announce drains once, on the transition into held. `queueMayDrainAfterReplay` treats a held run as drainable.
- A team whose manager has ended its turn (`rootsWorking = 0`) takes a message at once.
- While the agent is producing its reply, a typed message queues whatever background work is out. Background sub-agents and a detached command (`subagentsOut`, `shellsOpen`) park the agent only once its reply is over (its terminal item ended `streaming`, the turn is `held`, or — in a team — the daemon stopped counting the held lead in `rootsWorking`): the daemon counts them across turn boundaries, so they never decide a send mid-reply, here or in `queueMayDrainAfterReplay`. An agent blocked in its own wait on sub-agents is parked by the daemon's word, not the renderer's: claude's held turn, codex's `wait` (announced as `turn_held`), a manager's `await_agent` (`awaitingCalls`), each kept for a short gap after the wait returns so a polling agent is not read as busy between two waits.
- A send clears the composer first. `restoreUnsent` puts unsent content back into the thread it came from, never over newer typing.
- A message a turn accepted and then dropped (`unsentMessages` on a `system` row, paired by the user row's `deliveryId`) is drawn "Not sent" with Resend, quoting the CLI's `unsentReason` when it refused the message; the rules live in `chats/not-sent-messages.ts`. A `message_agent` "delivered" row whose `followUpId` a later "not delivered" row names draws nothing (`callFollowUpId`). Nothing is sent again unasked. Only a LIVE marker restores, and only into an empty composer (`restoreIntoEmpty`); a callee's message is never restored, since the composer feeds the run's lead; Resend goes through the queue with `followUpDelivery`'s kick, and a callee's message back to its own call (`callTargetOf`), never to the lead. A marker whose messages are all on screen draws nothing itself.
- Send waits for `useAttachments().reading` and for uploads (`attachBusy`). Each read and upload carries its owning thread.
- "Send now" checks the mid-turn channel of the agent the message lands in (the run's lead, `runLeadOf` over `RunDto.currentLead`), never `activeRun.agentKind` alone (it is null for team runs).
- A Send-now press POSTs `backgroundWork: 'ask'`. A message that would cancel running sub-agents (cursor, sub-agent sessions) is refused `FOLLOW_UP_CANCELS_SUBAGENTS`, which opens the confirm (`subagentCancelAsk`) with the daemon's sentence; confirming POSTs `'cancel'`, and the question goes when its message leaves the queue.
- The drain POSTs `waitForAnswer: true`. While a card a new turn would retire waits for the user, the daemon refuses it `RUN_AWAITS_ANSWER`: the message stays queued, no banner, and the answer's own turn ending fires the drain again. A Send-now press carries no such term, since pressing it is the user moving on from the card.
- Queue drag: call `preventDefault` on `dragover` first, then decide what to move. Never return early over the dragged row.
- The paperclip is an `<input type="file">`, which also works on a phone. A non-image file becomes its path. On a phone it is uploaded first (`POST /v1/chats/uploads`).
- A pasted non-image file becomes its path (`chats/paste-file-paths.ts`). Insert it with `execCommand('insertText')`. Give back a paste you cannot replace.
- A chat in a task's worktree re-creates it once on `INVALID_CWD` and resends — a message, a Retry or an Apply (`chats/task-worktree.ts`). That is the card's own chat, and any chat whose folder the card records as its `worktreePath` (an analysis of the card's thread runs there); the folder's last segment names the only card asked. While the worktree is cut again the message is drawn as the user's own row at the transcript's end (`chats/pending-send.ts`): per window, never persisted, counted in no statistic, gone at the send's settle or once the daemon's row is in the window.

**Run status:**

- `RunStatusKind` and `RUN_STATUS_META` (`chats/run-status.tsx`) are the one vocabulary for every surface.
- `held` and `needs-input` are derived in the renderer and never stored. There is no `idle`.
- A team run's badge stays `running` while any call it started works, its leads idle or not. The readings the notification rules watch read a `running` team run whose leads have all ended their turns (`rootsIdle`) as `completed` (`teamLeadsDone`), and that run's ending is not held for a settle (`notificationSettling`): the Manager's reply is announced while its calls run on, unless the idle announce carries `hostTurn` (a turn only a running monitor's output woke), which counts as a quiet ending like a settle's. The daemon marks it unread on the same transition (`readAttention`, `rootsWorking` dropping to 0 over a `running` run), and announces a pass's terminal status before its leads go idle, so a pass ending is never read as a reply.
- `displayRunStatus` applies a fixed rank order. A background sub-agent out (`subagentsOut`) reads `running` over a held or a `completed` row; a background command out (`shellsRunning`) reads `held` over a `completed` row. Neither is passed to the notification readings (`agentRunStatus`, `unfocusedAgentStoppedStatus`), which the notification rules watch.
- `BlockStatus` is never printed verbatim. Translate it through `RUN_STATUS_OF`.
- `settled-status.ts`: only a live terminal item mirrors its status onto the run row. A replay takes one reading from its last item (`reconcileFromTail`).
- A `turn_complete` stamped `insideTurn` is not terminal. Never mirror it.
- `RunStatusEvent.noTerminalItem` lets `use-chat-run.ts` end `streaming` and drain the queue.
- A settle arriving after the open run's live terminal row ends `streaming` too, without a second drain (`liveTurnEndedRef`): a stray `running` can land between the two. After-turn bookkeeping rows (`isAfterTurnBookkeeping`) do not count as the run resuming.
- A run settling is a moment, not a flag. `settleMomentOf` reads the last terminal item first and the row's `updatedAt` only as a fallback. `subagentSpokeSince` and `taskCardIsLive` use that moment. A sub-agent's own status (`subagentBlockStatus`) reads no run state at all: its rows, else the daemon's whole-conversation reading (`daemonSubagentReadings` + `applyDaemonReadings` in `durable-transcript.ts`), which is the only place a missing ending is resolved.
- A null activity means "working, nothing named". Both live rows fall back to the one `STANDING_ACTIVITY`. `WORKING_WORDS[0]` is its twin.
- Live clocks count from durable-row timestamps, never from their own mount.
- The chat live-plane key is `CHAT_LIVE_KEY` (`liveTextKey`), never the daemon's owner key `agent`. Node id is the discriminator.
- `chats/geniro-tool.ts` is the one place host tool names are matched. A composing card of an unknown kind draws nothing.
- While a turn runs there is one clock, the worked total, and no second elapsed-time readout beside it.
- A chat's open turn starts at the user's message, else at the agent's first reply, reasoning or tool call after the last ending (a stretch the CLI runs by itself; `opensOffTurnStretch`, twin of `ItemDao.openTurnStartedAt`), moved back to when the daemon saw it wake (`RunStateDto.turnStartedAt`, `withDurableChatTurn`). Its holds, ended and standing, are the daemon's (`turnHeldMs`, `turnHeldSince`); the live announce stands in only while the re-read is out.
- Turn duration uses the CLI's own figure where one exists. A thread total is the sum of its turns, never `updatedAt - createdAt`.
- A team's header clock (`clockMs`) is one formula for any number of open turns: the union of working stretches minus `pausedSpans`, the time a card waited on the user with no background shell or delegate running. Both come from the daemon's totals route (`utils/active-spans.ts`). It is re-read when the run's `awaiting`, shells-out or delegates-out flip, or a row they are folded from lands (`movesPausedSpans`). Never fold them from loaded `items`.

**Calls, instances and delegates:**

- A team run's agents come from the run's snapshot (`GET /v1/teams/runs/:runId/team`), never the library. The new-run trigger chip is the exception.
- A call waiting under its run's active-turn cap reads `queued`, never running — on its card, in the agents panel and on the working-agents card (`CallBlockEntry.queued`, folded from the executor's `queuedCall` row; its first status row ends it; off the page, run state's `RunCallState.queued`). `queued` is unsettled and is not working.
- A call continuing an earlier call's `thread` is the same instance.
- A call a callee made is drawn as a card inside its caller's card, never in the run's own flow: the claim pass files it under the call its `inCall` names, else under the newest call of its `callerConversationId` chain (`parentCallOf`, twin of the daemon's), and the parent's status, result and readings are read from its own rows only (`CallShell.nested`). Both surfaces resolve chains through `resolveCallChains` and match through `callIds`. A root page never carries such a call's rows, so a folded caller card holds no card for it: a callee of a call run state reads as running whose card is not in the window draws no live or working row (`withLiveText`'s `daemonOpenCalls`, from `runningCallCallees`); the working-agents card narrates it.
- The working-agents card (`chats/working-agents-card.tsx`, a team run's agents at the bottom right of the transcript, directly above the chips line) lists one row per conversation (`workingAgentRows`): run state's `calls` over the whole run, corrected only by what the window has seen move since that read. It floats in a hanging row after the transcript, so it never reflows the text. Its first view is a small square, whatever the run is doing: one lead face (`leadWorkingAgent`: whoever works, then the other live conversations, then an unseen failure, then the rest — user decision: the face is who is out now, and a failure leads only once nobody is live) with `+N` counting the other live conversations (the other conversations when none is live), two of one agent as two, a ring that spins only while a call is `working`, a red dot for a failure not yet seen, faded once nobody is live and no failure is unseen. Hover or keyboard focus opens a `HoverPopover` over the square, and a press pins that same panel (a press outside, a second press or Escape closes it; there is no other unfolded view). It lists every current run (each live conversation, a queued one included) as a card of its own, never grouped by agent (user decision: three runs of one QA are three cards), with the callee's face and name, its state (`waiting` for a callee holding its call for calls of its own, naming whom from run state's `callerConversationId`), clock, caller, title and three newest messages, the shelf's own status counters beside the name counting only that conversation's commands, monitors, delegates and task list (`conversation-work.ts`, filed by every call id of its chain; a press inside them never opens the conversation), under a live summary and over a fold of what has settled (`working-agents:finished`, per thread), each read as it opens; each seen failure is remembered per thread (`working-agents:failure-seen:<callId>`, written while the panel is open). A run reads the callee's newest three own messages from `GET /v1/chats/:runId/calls/:callId/messages` (`use-recent-call-messages.ts`), never a page of the conversation, draws each held to ten lines (`ClampedText`, Show more unfolds it in place) on a line down the left in the state's colour, the newest brightest, and takes live rows straight off the socket, since the transcript's stream stops at the tail. A press on a run, live or settled, closes the panel and opens its whole conversation in a popup, whose Show in chat is `openCall`. The popup is `call-conversation-dialog.tsx`: its own rows, read through `use-call-pages.ts` (the loader the opened cards use too) and folded by `useDurableTranscript`, so a call off the window is read without moving the window; a call the callee made from that conversation is drawn as a card inside it, and its live rows are taken too (`callsInside`).
- Never pool every instance's work into one list. Rows are filed by `callId`. Content whose call is not loaded becomes an inferred instance.
- A main conversation exists only when there is a `status` row with no `callId`. Never infer one by counting turns.
- Never time a wait from the agent's last row.
- The call-message queue is keyed by call block id, because call ids restart per pass. It retries only `CALL_MESSAGE_REFUSED`. A message row (`role === 'user'`) is never the block's summary or result.
- The prompt-cache badge threshold is a twin of the daemon's `CACHE_EXPIRED` rule, pinned by a spec.
- A call card's line, its shut band, its tasks chip and the agents panel's instance row read the callee's own thread (`callBlockActivity`, `callBlockSummary`, `callBlockTasks`), never a sub-agent's: several run at once, each with its own card.
- With no tool of its own running, or once its turn ended, a callee with sub-agents out reads the still `<member> is waiting for N sub-agents` (`callBlockSubagentsRunning`): its blocks in the window as each reads, plus the delegates the daemon says run in this conversation with no block there (`offWindowSubagents`, laid on by `applyDaemonReadings`). A shut band showing the callee's words carries it as a chip beside them. A held turn writes no row, so that card stays `running`; `waiting` needs the turn's terminal row.
- A delegate's generic title is its id tail. `resultIsOwn` is decided structurally, never by matching a CLI's prose. A `SendMessage` is not a delegation (`isAgentToolName`).
- Shells listed in the panel are the main thread's only, dropped at the item (`subagentIdOf`).

**Thread analysis** (`analysis-dialog.tsx`, `use-thread-analysis.ts`):

- A thread's analysis is never a sidebar row and never a search hit. Its home is its popup over the source (`AnalysisDialog`), where it is read, answered, applied and replaced; the Analyze button opens the popup when one exists, else the setup dialog, and marks it (tint; a dot while working or waiting on you). The popup and the setup dialog close when their thread is deleted (the analysis went with it).
- A direct link (`#chats/<id>`) or the processes panel can still open an analysis as the thread on screen; its header's "Analyzing: <source> ↗" chip (`analysisSourceOf`) is kept for exactly that way back.
- The popup and the builder's chat dock draw their conversation with the chat screen (`Chats` with `hosted`, above): every chip, label, queue and shelf a thread has, they have.
- A team's analysis setup dialog asks the daemon whether the picked agent and model share the members' vendor (`checkTeamAnalysisJudge`) and, when most members do, says a judge marks its own vendor high and offers the other agents.
- A team's analysis ("Analyze & improve…", the open team's overflow menu) uses the same two components, each given a `subject` of kind `team`: `AnalyzeThreadDialog` (plus how many latest runs to read, twin bound `MAX_ANALYSED_RUNS`) and `AnalysisDialog` (its team analysis read by `GET /v1/teams/:slug/analysis`, never the builder chat's). The menu opens the popup when one exists, else the setup dialog; both close when the builder leaves that team, and a turn of the analysis settling re-reads the team (`reloadAfterAgentEdit`), since an applied item changes the file. Every "is this an analysis" check is `isAnalysisRun` (either column). The builder's chat dock posts the same recommendations card (its agent reads the team's latest runs), applied by the chat screen's own `applyRecommendations`.

**Run waterfall** (`chats/run-waterfall*.ts(x)`, the "Where the run went" popup, opened from the agents panel's control row straight into its full view):

- Every span, its `seq` (what "Open in chat" jumps to) and the critical path come from the daemon's waterfall route; the renderer never folds them from loaded `items`.
- `blocksCaller` (`run-waterfall-geometry.ts`) is a twin of the daemon's critical-path rule in `v1/agents/utils/thread-timeline.ts`: any call not `async` holds its caller. Change one, change the other.
- The full view reads ONE state (zoom window, selected mark, focused agent); the tables count a turn's time where it overlaps the window and its money where it ended (`run-waterfall-breakdown.ts`).
- It opens as a SEQUENCE DIAGRAM (`run-waterfall-sequence.tsx` over `sequenceLayout` in `run-waterfall-sequence-layout.ts`; Calls / Lanes, `geniro.waterfall.lanes`): a column per agent, time running down, a numbered horizontal arrow per call (numbered by when it was made) from the caller's turn at that moment and a dashed one back under the same number for a call its caller waited on (`blocksCaller`). Arrows are one neutral colour (lit or failed aside); agent colours stay on the columns and bars. Turns of one agent running at once are stacked `NEST` pixels apart, and calls one agent made within `SEQUENCE_PARALLEL_MS` with no other arrow between are framed as "N at once" (`SequenceLayout.groups`). Every arrow has a row of its own at least `SEQUENCE_ROW_MIN` below the last, and an event-free stretch over `SEQUENCE_BREAK_MS` is drawn as a short break, so nothing overlaps. Pointing at or selecting a call lights the turns that answered it (`RunWaterfallTurn.callId`); a turn lights the calls it made.
- In Lanes, every call is drawn as a rounded line between its lanes (`CallLinks`, under the bars, measured off the DOM after layout): out of the caller's turn at the moment of the call, bending into the start of the turn that answered it — the daemon's `RunWaterfallTurn.callId`, overlap only for a run recorded before turns named their call — and, for a call its caller waited on (`blocksCaller`), dotted back into the caller at the moment it returned. Lines stay quiet until the pointer is over a turn or call, which lights that mark's own links (a selected call keeps its lit); the Links switch (`geniro.waterfall.callLinks`) keeps only the selected call's.
- The lanes are drawn `scale` card widths wide (`GANTT_SCALES`, 2× to start) and scroll sideways under the pinned name column; ⌘/Ctrl + wheel steps the scale around the pointer.

**Cards (agent-drawn payloads):**

- Each card kind has a twin parser (`*-payload.ts`) of the daemon's schema.
- Agent buttons (`action-chip.tsx`, `action-card.tsx`, `use-chat-actions.ts`): what is pressable is run state's `actions`, never a fold of the window. A press refused `RUN_BUSY` is retried by the hook (same seq, same `clientMessageId`) once the turn settles, never through the composer queue, which would send the text without recording the press. The shelf group sits after the stage chip, ahead of the running team chip. A team run presses through the team routes (the hook takes whichever API the open run needs; the chat route refuses a team run), and its busy refusals are asked again on `ACTION_RETRY_MS`, since its run state opens no turn. A button whose `publisher.agentLabel` is set (another team agent's) says so: `· <agent>` on the chip, `from <agent>` on the card, linking to its call's card, which it opens (`ChatActions.openCall`: the `call:<blockId>` thread flag, then a reveal, paging first when the card is off the window; the working-agents card's Show in chat does the same). A stop button lists what it stops (`spec.targets`: command and pid, plus what these start by the press) on its card and in the chip's tooltip before it is pressed, and its status reads `stopped` or `already stopped` (`alreadyStopped`).
- A command action opens `CommandConfirmDialog` from the chip, the card and the overflow row alike (the confirm state lives in `useChatActions`: `press` only sets `confirming`; one dialog per surface in `ChatProviders`), showing the exact argv, one quoted token per argument, and the folder. Nothing is sent until "Run command". The card shows the command (`Runs:`) before and after the press and, once it ends, its last run's status (exit code or error) even while the button stays live — a failed or reusable command stays pressable — plus a folded `Output` with the tail (`outputTail`, drawn through `AnsiText`, fold kept per thread). Only a message press starts a turn (`pressStartsTurn`), so only it raises a surface's working flag (the builder dock's).
- A recommendations card's knowledge items (`K-`) sit in a group of their own. A move's two sides are ticked and unticked together and each names the other (`movePartnerOf`), since the daemon refuses an Apply of one (`MOVE_SPLIT`).
- A team card states the population beside the sample (`data-slot="analysis-population"`: the runs read of how many the team has, with the average over all of them) and in the projection's footnote, drawn from `teamStats.population`, which the daemon measures over every ledger row; never folded here. An external item (`X-`) sits in a group of its own, "Other repositories": applying it files a board card, so its outcome note names that card.
- `isCardEntry` (`transcript-groups.ts`) is the one predicate over card kinds. Never write a `'task-list' || …` chain.
- A new card tool also goes in `GENIRO_TOOL_NAMES`, which the compiler cannot check.
- Findings keep the agent's order.
- Charts key rows by position (`s0`, `s1`, …), never by series name. `null` stays a gap.
- Metrics colour a delta by stated sentiment, never by sign.
- The gallery loads through `LocalImageLoaderContext`. It does that sequentially and only while open, releases images on fold, and uses no lightbox of its own. A picture named by URL arrives already downloaded by the daemon (a local path, with the URL as `source`); the renderer never fetches one. Each card shows its caption and description under the picture in one horizontally scrolling strip, and the viewer shows the description too.
- Dynamic workflow: the roster is taken whole. A launch is recognised by a `workflow_info` row, never by tool name.
- `propose_patch` renders as an ordinary approval card (`diff-view.tsx` `editDiffOf`). Its tool-name literal is a twin. `save_project_instructions` does too, drawing the daemon's unified hunks and the `P-` ids it carries out (`projectInstructionsCardOf`, `UnifiedDiff`) while its hazard scan reads the whole old and new text; the verdict row's `outcome` (`not-saved` draws "✗ Not saved") and `answer` say what Apply did, for a patch too (`hostPatchVerdict`). The card marks the words that changed inside paired lines (`UnifiedDiff` `wordLevel`). Both proposals are `'patch'` in `GENIRO_TOOLS` (`isProposedChange`); a refused `save_project_instructions` call keeps its row (`transcript-groups.ts`).
- A plan or question row that cannot be parsed falls back to the permission body.
- `propose_team` draws `TeamProposalCard` from its `approval_request` row (`team-proposal-payload.ts`, twin of the daemon's `TeamProposalSchema` / `TeamDiff` / `TeamProposalOutcome`); an unreadable payload falls back to the permission body. The agent's `summary` and `reason` (a change sent as edits, from any chat, always carries both) are drawn as its own words under the title. Saved / not saved is the verdict row's `outcome`, never the agent-bound message. A card whose team half changes nothing (`diff.identical`) says so in its badge and its saved line, and offers Open project instead of Open team where the surface can open one (`onOpenProject`; the project's own chat, already on that page, cannot). Its Approve gate scans every string the card can draw, raw (`proposalScanText`), the diff's old side included. `components/stage-graph-flow.tsx`, `lazy()` behind `StageGraph`, is the only chat-reachable importer of `@xyflow/react` and `elkjs`. The card draws the members (each with its public role and the blocks addressed to it, never its private instructions), the trigger member and the stages, and — as sections of their own beside the shared team, each a `UnifiedDiff` — a project's overlay and a project's instructions the same Approve writes (`overlay`, `project`, each read tolerantly and dropped whole when malformed), its approval line naming every file it changes; a proposal recorded in the node-graph shape is read through `teamFromLegacyGraph`. The change list comes first and is open; the members and the stage texts start folded, a fold still showing the members and stages a replacement adds or changes (`previewMarks`, `stageMarksOf`) and saying how many more it holds; and a long changed value is drawn as its two sides, which the daemon quotes from where they differ (`quoteTextChange`). The card stands across turns, so it counts as needs-input only while no turn streams (`standsAcrossTurns`, the daemon's `awaitingOf`).
- The plan and team cards share their note field and its send rule (`chats/verdict-note.tsx`); the hazard gate is `chats/text-hazard-gate.tsx`, shared with the permission card.
- A question card is drawn from the row's `questions`, never from a CLI's tool name. `secret` masks the field.
- `useOneShotVerdict` is the one verdict-timing contract.
- Only the latest task-list card shows its rows. Fold state derived from data takes a press override (`useThreadOverride`). Never seed derived open state into `useState`.
- Approval cards scan for text hazards (`chats/text-hazards.ts`):
  - Bidi or invisible characters gate Approve until acknowledged.
  - The tick resets when the content changes. Deny is never gated.
  - The banner names codepoints and never prints the character.
  - Join scanned parts with spaces, never newlines. Ordinary emoji are exempt.

**Artifacts** (`published-artifact.ts`, `artifact-frame.tsx`):

- The frame's sandbox is the root `AGENTS.md`'s (_Constraints_, the artifact page's outbound call). The page is served by the daemon, never put in `srcdoc`, and loaded with `referrerPolicy="no-referrer"`.
- Into the frame go theme tokens and, once the page announces itself, the answers kept for it (`ArtifactAnswersContext`, provided by `Chats` with `ArtifactUrlContext`); out of it come its height, a link, a click on one of its own `data:` pictures — the picture or a link to it (shown in `ImageViewer`), an Escape the page did not act on (`closeTopEscapeLayer`, since a key pressed in the frame never reaches the renderer's document), a folder button's path (`window.geniro.openArtifactPath`, desktop only, only on a fresh click — `navigator.userActivation` — one per `OPEN_PATH_GAP_MS`, its failure shown under the frame in the frame's own words) and each answer the page changes (`answer`, with the page's save count, sent as a batch of changes after `ANSWERS_SAVE_DELAY_MS` and on unmount, sent again after `ANSWERS_RETRY_MS` when refused; the merged set comes back to the saving frame with the counts it confirmed, and `ArtifactAnswersStore` hands it to every other frame of the page). All by `postMessage`, filtered on `event.source`; the picture, the Escape, the folder and the answers only while the frame holds focus (`document.activeElement`), so a page cannot act on the app unprompted.
- Key the frame by `${artifactId}:${version}`.
- A `show_artifact` row is never claimed into a call card (`UNCLAIMABLE_KINDS`): a callee's page is drawn in the main flow, and the newest-card sweep spans the whole run.
- The panel list comes from `GET /v1/chats/:runId/artifacts` and answers empty on a run mismatch.
- A saved file bakes in the theme and restates the CSP as `ARTIFACT_FILE_CSP`, a twin of the daemon's `artifact-page.ts`. Insert them into a parsed `<head>` (`DOMParser`), never by text splice.
- A saved file must look like the framed page. The daemon's raw reading (`renderArtifactDocument`) carries the frame's base style (font, padding, box-sizing, centring) but not its transparent ground, and the export supplies the ground.
- Links are opened by the host, only for `http:`, `https:` and `mailto:` (`shared/link-schemes.ts`); an inline page's go to the chat browser by the chat-link rule, the full-screen dialog's always to the system browser (its page hides the pane). Main's `guardArtifactFrameNavigations` cancels any frame navigation away from the artifact route.
- Share (`artifact-share.tsx` over `use-artifact-sharing.ts`, provided as `ArtifactShareContext` by `Chats`): beside Save on the card and in the dialog header; null on a paired phone, which draws nothing. A press sends the current `themeVars()`. The link (key in its `#fragment`) is read from the daemon when the panel opens, never kept in the list — and only for a share that was uploaded (`sharedVersion > 0`). A share waiting on sign-in (`awaiting-sign-in`) tints the icon `text-warning` and shows "Sign-in required (<cli>)" with `SignInAction` (`components/sign-in-action.tsx` over `use-share-sign-in.ts`, polling like `use-cli-login.ts` but never sharing its wire) in the panel and as a strip under the card; the daemon retries after the sign-in and announces `shares_changed`, which re-reads the list (`daemon-client.ts` `onSharesChanged`). Settings → Sharing checks a place only on Check, never on open. Settings → Sharing (`settings/sharing.tsx`) edits the places, the agents switch and the auto-revoke window, and lists every shared page with the moment it was first shared, Copy link, Revoke and Revoke all.

**Shells** (`chats/shell-activity.ts`):

- Detached state comes from the daemon's `shell_open`/`shell_info` rows, never from a text match. `readLaunchHandle` stays strict and anchored at the start of the reply.
- A failed kill is not a kill.
- Foreground commands are gated on `isSettledRunStatus`. Detached commands are not.
- The whole gate lives in `Chats.tsx`'s `shellsByAgent`, and every count flattens that map.
- `shell_info` and `workflow_info` rows are skipped outright in `groupTranscript`.

**The RING is drawn from three sources** in strict recency order (`chatContextFigures`): the live delta, then `Run.contextTokens`, then the last settled turn of the loaded transcript. `useContextReadings` is the last fallback.

- Read the live value directly, never through `agents[0]`.
- `rememberRunContext` mirrors live readings onto the run-row copy, only when the value changed and is positive.
- A team's composer ring is its lead's (`runLeadOf`: `RunDto.currentLead`, else the trigger's member). Otherwise it is null. A relay run also shows who leads as the shelf's `LeadChip`, and draws every member's rows with their identity (no `soloNodeId`).
- A member's ring order is `cardContextOf`. The nodes route is fetched on run open and reconnect only (`reconnectNonce`), and its map is cleared on entry.
- A geniro-performed compaction (cursor) invalidates earlier readings. It resets the fold (`endsContextHistory`), clears the live plane and the mirrored row, and the ring shows empty with `awaitingReading`.
- The context panel is fetched on open and never polled. On a team run every readout branch carries `memberId`.
- Category colours are assigned by position, never by name.
- Money goes through `formatUsd` (a bound, for columns), `formatExactUsd` (an exact value, for standalone figures) or, for chart ticks only, `formatUsdAxis` (`stats/stats-format.ts`). Never add another format. A figure nothing measured is a dash or omitted, never `$0.00` or `0`.

**Composer and chips:**

- `directory-select` is the one directory chip.
- `config-dir-select` renders nothing (never a disabled chip) when the CLI's `configDirs[]` row names a reason. `EffortSelect` and `ContextWindowSelect` return null on an empty listing.
- `target-select`: a CLI this machine cannot run is shown disabled, never hidden. The current target is never refused.
- `BranchSelect` switches the checkout. `BranchValueSelect` only records a name.
- The composer's model and its axes are one control (`chats/model-settings-select.tsx`), with two levels. Do not go back to a row of separate chips.
- Axes show one loading row while listings load.
- Context windows and model parameters come from the CLI per (CLI, model), never from a table here. Choosing a model clears the window everywhere it is stored (`modelChangePatch`).
- Use `withModelParameter` for parameter edits. An inline spread writes `{id: undefined}`.
- Auto-compact bounds are twinned in `shared/contracts.ts`. `Settings.lastAutoCompactPercent` is one value, not one per CLI.
- Approval modes come from `GET /v1/capabilities` `approvals[]`, never from an allowlist of agent names. `requiresPass` and `withheld` modes are hidden. The current value is always kept.
- A mid-turn mode change refused with 409 `RUN_BUSY` shows `daemonErrorDetail`.

**Pull requests and changes:**

- A thread's pull requests are only those it opened or its CLI recorded it touching (the run wire's `pullRequests`, each marked `opened`; a task card shows only the opened ones), addressed by URL (`use-thread-pull-requests.ts` over `readPullRequestsByRef`). That hook is the only source for the panel, the sidebar and the shelf.
- A pull request's recorded outcome (`record_outcome`) is the daemon's `DisplayedPullRequest.outcome`, never folded from the window: the shelf chip draws its acceptance mark beside the number from it, and a `run_outcome` transcript row reads superseded once that outcome's seq is newer than its own (`RunOutcomesContext`). The row opens on a press into the agent's summary, every figure and the pull request's link row; its title and state come from the same `use-thread-pull-requests.ts` answers the panel draws (`RunOutcomes.resolved`), so a surface that asks none draws the number alone. `daemon-client.ts` carries it through every run-status announce. The agents panel's Pull requests section draws a pull request's whole outcome history under its row (`chats/pull-request-outcome-history.tsx`, folded per thread to the newest verdict), read from the daemon (`GET /v1/chats/:runId/outcomes`, `use-run-outcomes.ts` over `use-run-daemon-read.ts`, the one whole-run read the artifacts and team-notes lists share) and re-read when the newest outcome seq on the run's list moves.
- A team run's stage progress is the daemon's `RunDto.stages` (`StageProgress`), replaced whole by every `run_status.stages` (`run-status-row.ts`) — never folded from `stage_change` rows, so `n/m` and every status hold past the loaded window. The progress popup (`progress-dialog.tsx`, opened from the shelf's Progress chip and the agents panel's Goals row; its stage rows are `stage-entry.tsx`), the agents-panel Stages section (moves through `moveTeamRunStage` with `expectedRev`; a `409 STAGE_PROGRESS_CHANGED` keeps the dialog open) and the transcript row (`stage-change-row.tsx`, next-stage titles from `RunStagesContext`, provided once with the transcript's values (`TranscriptContexts`); a move's row reads superseded once its stage's `StageProgressEntry.rev` names a later move, a nudge's once the run's `rev` moved on) all draw it, and every stage graph is the one `components/stage-graph.tsx`: React Flow nodes laid out and routed by ELK (`stage-graph-layout.ts`, orthogonal wires, so a wire that skips stages runs beside them and never across a pill), drawn once its lazy chunk and the layout land, so a spec reading its pills awaits `__tests__/settle-stage-graphs.ts`. The board card's line reads `TaskDto.stage`; `use-board.ts` reloads on a stages-only announce for a run it holds.
- A sidebar row names the thread's newest resolved pull request among those it opened, and a touched one only for a thread that opened none — never in place of an opened one that did not resolve (`rowPullRequestOf`).
- A ref GitHub could not answer still draws a row.
- The renderer replaces the run's list wholesale on each announce.
- Do not reintroduce a branch-scoped pull-request list.
- `keepFresherRows`: a pin, colour or pull-request list written without moving `updatedAt` is decided by the listing. So are the live readings (`awaiting`, `holdingFor`, the shell/sub-agent/call counts), unless an announce of them arrived while the listing was in flight: no write stamps them, and an announce's `at` always postdates the row's `updatedAt`.
- "Changed since this chat started" uses `upstreamBaseSince` and excludes what a pull brought in. The header names the ref and claims no cause.
- A folder on one of the thread's open pull requests is measured from that pull request's base branch, so a stacked branch lists what GitHub lists (`changesPullRequestsOf` → `getChangesSince`'s fourth argument, re-read when GitHub's answer lands; `folderPullRequest` in main keeps those of the `origin` repository and matches the checked-out branch, else the head commit, else, for a detached worktree, a commit behind or past that head that is not already on its base). Without a match, a fetched base ref or a base past the start, the read falls back to the default branch, then the start. A worktree's dialog section names its own ref. A read past `MAX_CHANGES` draws `N+` on the chip, and its line counts cover only the files read.
- The changes chip and dialog also count the worktrees the thread's agents made (`use-run-work-folders.ts`). A worktree is the thread's only if all three hold: main lists it (`listWorktrees(cwd, startedAtMs)`: a linked worktree made after the thread started — birth of its `.git` link file — never the main checkout and never a geniro task worktree, the direct children of `worktreesRoot()`), and the run's own tool calls name it (`POST /v1/chats/:runId/named-paths`). Each is read as a work folder (`getChangesSince(…, true)`: from where its history meets the start, past what upstream already has). Found folders are only ever added; each lists in its own dialog section. Listings go through `worktree-list.ts` (see _Git_ in `apps/ui/src/main/AGENTS.md`).

**Thread UI memory** (`chats/thread-ui-memory.ts`):

- Folds are remembered per thread, keyed by the row they belong to (`tools:<group id>`, `tool:<call id>`, …), never by an index.
- Only a press writes.
- Which hook remembers a boolean, in a thread or outside one: `.claude/rules/renderer-components.md` → _The one home for…_.
- Two things are deliberately not per thread: the context readout's drill-downs and the builder palette.
- The agents panel is always rendered when a chat is open and starts folded per thread. Nothing but its per-thread fold shows or hides it: no show/hide toggle, no header button, no auto-open on an artifact.
- The browser pane (`chats/browser/`) is a column of its own between the transcript and the agents panel, opened per thread by the agents panel's globe; it never takes the agents panel's place. It draws a placeholder that main covers with the chat's active tab (`browserSetBounds`), so every overlay primitive (`Dialog`, `Menu` — and `ContextMenu` and `Select` through it — and `Popover`) reports while it is open (`components/browser-occlusion.ts`), and while any is open anywhere the pane swaps the view for a still (`browserCapture`) — a new overlay that skips this is drawn under the page. The one exception is the pane's own full-screen view (`BrowserColumn`), a `Dialog` with `coversBrowser={false}` that hosts the view itself while the column's pane steps aside. Open in new window moves the whole browser into a window of its own whose renderer draws only `BrowserPane` with `windowed` (`chats/browser/browser-window-root.tsx`, chosen in `main.tsx` before `App` loads); the docked pane keeps the tab strip and says the browser is in its own window, with Show window and Dock back. It opens by itself only for a tab an agent just opened (`BrowserStateEvent.agentOpenedTabId`), once, and when the browser docks back from its own window (`dockedBack`, so a column closed during the pop-out comes back); later agent actions only mark the globe. Every chat link (`useChatLink`, `ChatLink`, `ChatPanelLinkRow` in `chats/browser/chat-link.tsx`) opens an `http(s)` address in that pane on a plain click only while the pane is open, else in the system browser; its right-click menu offers both browsers and Copy link. A tab shows its name (`BrowserTabState.name`, else the page title, which stays in its tooltip) beside a colour dot that opens its colour and Rename menu (`components/color-rename-menu.tsx`, shared with the terminal's tabs; double-click the name renames too, through `TabStrip`'s `leading`, `editor` and `onDoubleClick`); the user's rename or recolour goes through `browserLabelTab` and main keeps it from every later agent label, field by field. On a paired device there is no pane and the globe says "The browser runs on your Mac". Tabs and cookies are each thread's own; the pane holds no browser state of its own beyond what main reports (`onBrowserState`).
- The agents panel's column holds only what belongs to the thread: its agents, teams, pull requests and pages. The run waterfall, the processes readout and the notes open in popups from its control row (and rail), never as sections in the column; the waterfall's and processes' reads are gated on that popup being open (`onPopupChange`), never on the panel being unfolded.
- The progress popup (`chats/progress-dialog.tsx`: a team run's stages, the goals with the user's add, edit, close and reopen, and the thread's outcome) is the chat screen's too: the shelf's Progress chip and the agents panel's Goals row open it. The chip stands whenever the thread has stages, goals or an outcome, or the user can add a goal, and reads "Set goals" — the one status counter with words — while the user can add one and none is set; moving a stage stays in the panel's Stages section.
- The notes popup (`chats/notes-dialog.tsx`: the user's own notes, then a team run's team notes) is the chat screen's, not the panel's: the control row's notes button, the shelf's Team notes chip and a `team_note` row in the transcript (`TeamNoteOpenContext`) all open it, the last two on the pressed note.
- Read the MCP listing only when its disclosure opens (`mcpOpen`).

**Fast actions** (`chats/fast-action-bar.tsx`, `settings/fast-actions.tsx`, `Settings.fastActions`):

- An action is a name and a description, nothing else. A press appends the description to the message box. It sends nothing and touches no chip. Never add run setup to an action.
- `persistFastActions` calls `window.geniro.updateSettings` directly, not the screen's `persist`, which swallows the rejection the rollback needs.
- The editor refuses locally whatever `settingsPatchSchema` would refuse. That boundary throws on the whole patch.

**Run configurations** (`chats/run-config.ts`, `new-chat-button.tsx`, `run-config-picker.tsx`, `projects/project-run-configs.tsx`, `Settings.runConfigs`):

- Named new-chat setups. Applying one seeds the new-chat screen and switches the branch. It sends nothing.
- Never fold run configurations and fast actions together. Doing so once deleted users' saved configurations.
- Every configuration belongs to a project (`RunConfig.projectId`), and is kept on that project's page; Settings has no pane for them. One component (`RunConfigPicker`) and one key serve the project page (inline, scoped to the project, `onReorder` laid back into the whole list by `withSubsetOrder`) and the `+` menu's "New configuration…" dialog. `onApply` separates them. The `+` menu groups them by project (`groupRunConfigsByProject`, unbound last as "No project"); its "Manage configurations…" opens the composer's project, else the Projects gallery.
- The editor refuses a draft whose project is unset or no longer listed (`runConfigRefusal`); picking a project fills an empty folder with the project's.
- A configuration with no project (saved before they belonged to projects, or whose project was deleted — a delete keeps them and clears the id) is never deleted for it: the Projects screen binds one with no id to its folder's project by the daemon's rule (`useBindRunConfigsByFolder` over `GET /v1/projects/of-folder`, once per configuration per mount), and lists the rest under the gallery (`UnboundRunConfigs`) to move into a project or delete. Older builds drop a configuration carrying `projectId` (strict `runConfigSchema`).
- A team target writes none of the per-CLI choices. A `null` through those setters deletes the remembered entry.
- A branch refusal is a partial success: amber, through `composerNotice`, with no worktree redirect.
- `chats/composer-values.ts` reads foreign settings strings. Import it, never restate it.

**Session picker and config profiles:**

- The session picker searches every profile on the daemon. The fan-out is in the renderer, because profiles live in `settings.json`.
- The profile is carried on the row, and `resumeSession` takes it from the row.
- `partialReason` names unreadable profiles and a hit `SESSION_LIST_LIMIT`. There is no client-side filter. Do not reintroduce a folder-scope filter.
- The picker's agent is its own. `sessionProfiles` collapses to the default profile for a CLI with no config directory.
- A `ConfigProfile` is `{ id, name, agent, dir, color }`, and the directory is its identity. Two entries may not name one directory.
- A profile is offered to its own CLI only.
- The palette union is spelled in `shared/contracts.ts` (not derived from `RunGroupColor`). `GROUP_DOT_CLASS` stays an exhaustive `Record`.

**Looks redundant, is not.** Check here before cutting:

- The task list renders in both the transcript and the agents panel. The user confirmed they want both.
- The shelf's Tasks, Sub-agents and Terminals chips restate panel counts on purpose.
- The chat-list row is four lines. Do not delete one.
- The tail `ResizeObserver` watches the scroller itself as well as its children, because a shrinking viewport fires nothing else.
- The composer shelf reserves its height (`min-h-7`). Anything thread-produced worth reaching is another chip there, never a new band — with one exception (user decision): messages other threads sent that still wait for this thread's turn are drawn above the composer as `chats/waiting-messages-strip.tsx` (sender, why it waits, first lines, Deliver now and Remove), because a counter alone went unnoticed; the Waiting messages chip lists them all. The working-agents card floats over the transcript instead, holding what a chip cannot: a row per agent and its messages. Content chips (changes, pull requests, buttons, teams) run durable to volatile from the left; the status counters (Terminals, Monitors, Sub-agents, Waiting messages, Team notes, Tasks, Progress) are glyph + figure only in `ComposerShelf`'s right-anchored `status` cluster, volatile to durable so the right edge stays put.

**Do not reintroduce:**

- Landing-view centring on the window.
- The run's folder in the composer: it belongs in the header.
- A horizontal timeline strip, or a timeline inside `subagent-block.tsx`.
- Always-visible controls for an absent axis.
